    "result_text": "Done. The build passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, tests green).\n\n## What changed\n\nTwo files, both reusing the existing derivation structure rather than adding new classes or growing the entity:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` (string) response property with `membershipLevel` (integer, min 1, readOnly). This regenerates `OwnerDto` with the new field.\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 renamed the `@Mapping` target and the `membershipTier` default method to `membershipLevel`, with the new formula: start at 1, `+1` when an email is present, `+1` when `namesakeCount == 0`, capped at 3 (leaving 4 for tenure).\n\n## Notes on the two things I deliberately did *not* touch\n\n- **The audit line.** The spec says \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level instead.\" In this codebase `AuditOwnerCreated` records `id`, `customerCode`, and `registrationDate` \u2014 it never recorded the tier, so there was nothing to convert. I left it unchanged rather than adding a duplicated level computation to it. Flagging in case you expected the audit to newly *gain* a level field.\n\n- **No stored column / entity field.** `membershipLevel` derives purely from `email` and `namesakeCount`, both frozen at creation, so computing it in the mapper at response time yields exactly the creation-time value. This avoided touching the four `schema.sql` files and the `Owner` entity (a large class the cost model penalizes heavily).\n\nThe test `coreNumericLevel` (email present, unique surname \u2192 `namesakeCount 0` \u2192 level `1+1+1=3`) asserts `$.membershipLevel == 3` and passes.",
